and or may focus on the rebellion's  unsung hero but this galaxy might not be  as far far away from the Star Wars  mainstream as fans expect in the very  first episode of Andor viewers see  flashbacks to cassian's Childhood on  qunari his home planet the clips show  him Dawning combat gear and weapons to  investigate a crashed ship with the  other inhabitants of his village a  mission that ultimately proves  life-changing and not necessarily in the  best way it's interesting to see such a  young version of Cassian running around  with a spear in his hand but if you've  seen Rogue one you may have been  expecting it in the movie Cassian tells  jyn urso about his long history as a  freedom fighter have been in this fight  since I was six years old it emphasizes  just how much of his life has been spent  either on the run or in direct conflict  with the powers that be while the ship  he's investigating isn't Imperial and or  does make good on cassian's claim of  being a part of the good fight from a  very young age though the Star Wars  franchise has featured a ton of  different Starships over the decades  some have been iconic enough to stand  out anywhere even in a random Shipyard  on ferrics where Cassian is currently  living near the end of Andor season 1's  opening episode Cassian rummages around  in an old junker that he borrowed from  one of the yard's employees he plays it  cool but he's actually scrubbing  incriminating info from the ship's  records during a couple of exterior  shots of the ship viewers May recognize  a few of the other ships occupying the  yard one is unmistakably a y-wing  Starfighter a bomber commonly used by  the Rebel Alliance and first seen during  the run on the original Death Star the  faded yellow cockpit of the ship can be  seen clearly complete with the iconic  rotating turret y wings were first used  by the Republic during the Clone Wars so  it makes sense to see a stray one  junking it up on a planet like ferrics  the scene also shows what looks like a  variant of the t-47 airspeeder from The  Empire Strikes Back originally  manufactured as stunt Fighters t-47s  were modified for various purposes by  rebels throughout the Galactic Civil War  they became known as snow Speeders among  the Rebels the ship seen opposite the  y-wing in the Andor Shipyard isn't a  snowspeeder it's much longer in the back  than a normal t-47 but the cockpit and  the wing design are quite evocative one  of the most distinct aspects of the Star  Wars universe is its retro sci-fi Tech  aesthetic when the first film was  released in 1977 computers used vector  graphics as the franchise continued and  real world technology evolved Star Wars  played with different variants on its  original in-universe technology like how  the prequels employed more holographic  screens instead of the vectors and or  makes it clear from the get-go that its  inspiration comes fully from the  original old school Tech of the first  Star Wars films from the numerous  computer screens filled with hard lines  to the digital readouts on Luth and  rails binoculars the show fully Embraces  the Retro futuristic look it's similar  in many ways to the aesthetic of the  Mandalorian but with an early Imperial  era twist if you keep your eyes peeled  throughout and or season 1 you'll catch  a lot of little gadgets and gizmos that  have appeared in previous Star Wars  stories in cassian's flashbacks to the  ship crash on his Homeworld of qunari we  see how he came to be raised by Marva  Andor and taken far away into the Galaxy  it all centers around the Shipwreck  itself which Cassian investigates with a  party from his village things go sour  when some of the passengers turn out to  be both alive and hostile leading to a  violent confrontation who are these  Interlopers and where were they headed  it's not entirely clear but the familiar  logo on their uniforms gives some  indication on the sleeves of their  spacesuits you can make out the  segmented blue and white hexagon that  was the symbol of the separatist  Alliance during the Clone Wars there are  no battle droids to be seen aboard the  ship But Die Hard fans will know that  plenty of non-robotic beings gave their  lives to the separatist cause as well  this also might explain why the wounded  crewman immediately gets violent after  waking up the ship may have been coming  directly from a war zone though of  course that's no justification for his  actions when the corporate security  Agents come to ferricks to hunt Cassian  in episode 3 they come prepared not only  do they gear up with some major  Firepower but they drop down to the  planet's surface and ships that are  strikingly reminiscent of the Republic  gunships used in the Clone Wars the  iconic design was first seen an attack  of the Clones and it has remained a  staple of Star Wars ever since while  there are certainly differences between  the old gunships and the landing craft  seen in Andor their striking  similarities are interesting to say the  least in the wake of the Clone Wars The  Empire phases out a lot of the gear used  by the Clone Army old starfighters  become Tie fighters attes become atats  and the old clone gunships are nowhere  to be found it would make sense for a  private military company like the one  seen in Andor to purchase some of this  Surplus tech for its own purposes  perhaps even making some modifications  along the way that could explain the  ship design in episode 3 or maybe the  same Factor Simply Built some new models  after the war during their first meeting  in Andor episode 3 Luther rail asks how  Cassian managed to steal the untraceable  Imperial Tech he's bartering off  you just walk in like you belong to  steal from the empire  what do you need a uniform some dirty  hands and an imperial token his  explanation can be taken as a nod to the  many many times Rebels have successfully  infiltrated Imperial strongholds using  basic disguises throughout the franchise  it's a long and storied Trope worthy of  the reference whether it's Han and Luke  disguising themselves as Stormtroopers  on the Death Star or Obi-Wan Kenobi  sneaking around the Imperial Inquisitor  Citadel escorted by a double agent many  characters have walked right past the  Empire's defenses with little more than  courage and a cool hat Cassian himself  uses this exact tactic at the end of  Rogue one when he and Jen urso break  into the Imperial base on scarif to  steal the Death Star plans as such his  line about borrowing uniforms can also  be taken as a grim foreshadowing of his  eventual demise at the end of Andor  episode 3 we're treated to a beautifully  shot sequence that parallels cassian's  departure from qunari as a young boy  with his escape from ferrics with Luther  rail it's a powerful and haunting moment  showing the character's talent for  making it out by the skin of his teeth  the scene is tragic not only because it  emphasizes how Cassian must repeatedly  leave things behind but because it  foreshadows the one time he finally  doesn't make it out most people who  watch Andor will already know cassian's  ultimate fate it's particularly  affecting then to see that earlier in  his life Cassian was known for narrow  getaways so many different times he  almost meets his end only to narrowly  escape his wits take him far in his  struggle against totalitarianism but  even Cassian andor's luck must  eventually run out  foreign  [Music]
 

